   "id": "790a8015-3608-49b2-9f84-98947276dde5",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Motility Induced Phase Separation in 2D\n",
    "\n",
    "## Overview\n",
    "\n",
    "### Questions\n",
    "\n",
    "* How does **motility-induced phase separation (MIPS)** emerge in systems of purely repulsive active particles?\n",
    "\n",
    "### Objectives\n",
    "\n",
    "* Implement a 2D simulation of **Active Brownian Particles (ABPs)** to study MIPS.\n",
    "* Apply an OverdampedViscous integrator with rotational diffusion to explore activity-driven clustering.\n",
    "* Highlight the core mechanisms behind active phase separation.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "## Boilerplate Code"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 1,
   "id": "ad17a299-5bc3-497a-85c1-5a197dc0dbe3",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import hoomd\n",
    "import itertools\n",
    "import freud\n",
    "\n",
    "import gsd.hoomd\n",
    "\n",
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 2,
   "id": "d2459ab2-f60e-4b49-bbdb-aa56912c6881",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import os\n",
    "\n",
    "fn = os.path.join(os.getcwd(), \"lattice.gsd\")\n",
    "![ -e \"$fn\" ] && rm \"$fn\"\n",
    "fn = os.path.join(os.getcwd(), \"MIPS.gsd\")\n",
    "![ -e \"$fn\" ] && rm \"$fn\""
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "bd98d157-f536-4112-97f8-988d4609a016",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The `render` function in the next (hidden) cell will render a snapshot using **fresnel**. Find the source in the [hoomd-examples](https://github.com/glotzerlab/hoomd-examples) repository."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 3,
   "id": "a3abfe9b-3066-4698-b3e0-779253da5764",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# This is not intended as a full tutorial on fresnel - see the fresnel user\n",
    "# documentation (https://fresnel.readthedocs.io/) if you would like to learn more.\n",
    "\n",
    "import warnings\n",
    "\n",
    "import fresnel\n",
    "import IPython\n",
    "import packaging.version\n",
    "\n",
    "device = fresnel.Device()\n",
    "tracer = fresnel.tracer.Path(device=device, w=300, h=300)\n",
    "\n",
    "FRESNEL_MIN_VERSION = packaging.version.parse(\"0.13.0\")\n",
    "FRESNEL_MAX_VERSION = packaging.version.parse(\"0.14.0\")\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "def render(snapshot):\n",
    "    if ('version' not in dir(fresnel) or packaging.version.parse(\n",
    "            fresnel.version.version) < FRESNEL_MIN_VERSION\n",
    "            or packaging.version.parse(\n",
    "                fresnel.version.version) >= FRESNEL_MAX_VERSION):\n",
    "        warnings.warn(\n",
    "            f\"Unsupported fresnel version {fresnel.version.version} - expect errors.\"\n",
    "        )\n",
    "    central_color = fresnel.color.linear([252 / 255, 41 / 255, 0 / 255])\n",
    "\n",
    "    L = snapshot.configuration.box[0]\n",
    "    scene = fresnel.Scene(device)\n",
    "    geometry = fresnel.geometry.Sphere(scene,\n",
    "                                       N=len(snapshot.particles.position),\n",
    "                                       radius=0.5)\n",
    "    geometry.material = fresnel.material.Material(color=[0, 0, 0],\n",
    "                                                  roughness=0.5,\n",
    "                                                  primitive_color_mix=1.0)\n",
    "    geometry.position[:] = snapshot.particles.position[:]\n",
    "    geometry.color[snapshot.particles.typeid[:] == 0] = central_color\n",
    "    geometry.radius[snapshot.particles.typeid[:] == 0] = 0.3\n",
    "    geometry.outline_width = 0.04\n",
    "    box = fresnel.geometry.Box(scene, [L, L, L, 0, 0, 0], box_radius=.02)\n",
    "\n",
    "    scene.lights = [\n",
    "        fresnel.light.Light(direction=(0, 0, 1),\n",
    "                            color=(0.8, 0.8, 0.8),\n",
    "                            theta=np.pi),\n",
    "        fresnel.light.Light(direction=(1, 1, 1),\n",
    "                            color=(1.1, 1.1, 1.1),\n",
    "                            theta=np.pi / 3)\n",
    "    ]\n",
    "    scene.camera = fresnel.camera.Orthographic(position=(0, 0, L * 2),\n",
    "                                               look_at=(0, 0, 0),\n",
    "                                               up=(0, 1, 0),\n",
    "                                               height=L * 1.1)\n",
    "    scene.background_alpha = 1\n",
    "    scene.background_color = (1, 1, 1)\n",
    "    return IPython.display.Image(tracer.sample(scene, samples=500)._repr_png_())"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "72253c9e-f0cb-482b-9793-f7d1d341da72",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Introducing Motility Induced Phase Separation\n",
    "\n",
    "In this tutorial, we replicate much of the set up as in [the previous section](02-Active-Brownian-Particles.ipynb) where we introduced active Brownian particle simulations. Here, we now aim to understand an important collective behavior that colections of active particles may present called **motility-induced phase separation (MIPS)** where a system of purely repulsive active particles exhibits a phase transition to gas-liquid coexistance. MIPS systems provide clear visual confirmation of active behavior, as the emergence of distinct dense and dilute regions—absent in equivalent passive systems. \n",
    "\n",
    "For purely repulsive particles, the activity of the particle and the density of the system control how we may drive our system into a MIPS state."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "90b1c662-4e88-4799-8b8a-5828812df33c",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Set up the Simulation\n",
    "\n",
    "Here, we conduct our simulations in two dimensions, making it easier to observe clustering behavior, density fluctuations, and phase separation driven purely by activity and crowding effects. \n",
    "\n",
    "We will first initialize a system of spherical particles with a given particle density in a 2D array. We then save an initial snapshot of the current state of the system."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 4,
   "id": "65e584e6-6eb2-46aa-a3a9-3f093fe25bd5",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "rho = 0.3 # particle area density\n",
    "simseed = 1\n",
    "\n",
    "m = 50\n",
    "N_particles = m**2\n",
    "K = np.ceil(N_particles ** (1 / 2))\n",
    "L = (N_particles / rho)**(1 / 2)\n",
    "spacing = L / K\n",
    "x = np.linspace(-L / 2, L / 2, int(K), endpoint=False)\n",
    "\n",
    "position_2D = np.array(list(itertools.product(x, repeat=2))) + [spacing / 2, spacing / 2]\n",
    "position_3D = np.hstack([position_2D[:N_particles], np.zeros((N_particles, 1))])\n",
    "\n",
    "frame = gsd.hoomd.Frame()\n",
    "frame.particles.N = N_particles\n",
    "frame.particles.position = position_3D[0:N_particles, :]\n",
    "frame.particles.typeid = [0] * N_particles\n",
    "frame.configuration.box = [L, L, 0, 0, 0, 0,]\n",
    "frame.particles.types = ['A']\n",
    "frame.particles.mass = [1] * N_particles\n",
    "frame.particles.orientation = [[1, 0, 0, 0]] * N_particles\n",
    "\n",
    "with gsd.hoomd.open(name='lattice.gsd', mode='w') as f:\n",
    "    f.append(frame)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "4035780f-4170-4815-8cef-c065d4558e93",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Configure this simulation to run on CPU from this snapshot as we have in previous tutorials. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 5,
   "id": "d2d0189e-f7a8-44ee-aacf-3ac341fe5fa4",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "True. Lz = 0 in the simulation box.\n"
     ]
    },
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<IPython.core.display.Image object>"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 5,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "cpu = hoomd.device.CPU()\n",
    "sim = hoomd.Simulation(device=cpu, seed=simseed)\n",
    "state = sim.create_state_from_gsd(filename='lattice.gsd')\n",
    "\n",
    "render(sim.state.get_snapshot())"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "81343f7b-bdda-447a-8b3c-f369ea7806ef",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Defining Interactions in an Active Particle system"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "d8883a35-6204-4e09-99ec-ef31d5429f06",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Add a short range Lennard Jones (WCA) pair potential interactions between particles."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 7,
   "id": "462748ea-f39f-479f-8886-085dbecc7aaf",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "LJ_sigma = 1\n",
    "LJ_epsilon = 1\n",
    "\n",
    "cell = hoomd.md.nlist.Cell(buffer=0.4)\n",
    "wca = hoomd.md.pair.LJ(nlist=cell, default_r_cut=(2**(1/6))*LJ_sigma, default_r_on=0.0, mode='shift')\n",
    "wca.params[('A', 'A')] = {'sigma': LJ_sigma, 'epsilon': LJ_epsilon}\n",
    "\n",
    "integrator.forces.append(wca)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "7b2ad9c8-043e-407b-9007-1a55611b7e38",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Set up the OverdampedViscous integrator."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 8,
   "id": "6bc1838d-84d0-4323-bd03-db02e4a5bcd5",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "kT = 0\n",
    "gamma = 1\n",
    "\n",
    "filter_all = hoomd.filter.All()\n",
    "overdamped = hoomd.md.methods.OverdampedViscous(filter=filter_all)\n",
    "overdamped.gamma.default = gamma\n",
    "overdamped.gamma_r.default = [gamma,gamma,gamma]\n",
    "\n",
    "integrator = hoomd.md.Integrator(dt=0.001, integrate_rotational_dof=False)\n",
    "sim.operations.integrator = integrator\n",
    "integrator.methods.append(overdamped)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "90fb1f70-2ed3-4cd3-8bcb-2991b8103bf8",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Peclet number, activity and MIPS\n",
    "\n",
    "A key quantity that you may want to control in your simulations is the **Péclet number (Pe)**. This is a key dimensionless parameter in active matter that quantifies the relative strength of persistent active motion versus random thermal diffusion. It’s critical for determining whether MIPS occurs. For spherical ABPs in 2D this is typically defined as Pe = $v_{0} \sigma / \D_{r}$ where \n",
    "- $v_{0}$ = self-propulsion speed (active force per friction)\n",
    "- $\sigma$ = particle diameter (typically = 1 in reduced units)\n",
    "- and $D_{r}$ = rotational diffusion coefficient\n",
    "\n",
    "As you saw in the previous tutorial, in HOOMD-blue, the propulsion velocity is controlled by applying an active force to particles in the system. The relationship between these quantities is $v_{0} = F_{active} / \gamma$ where $\gamma$ is the translational drag coefficient (often = 1 in overdamped Brownian dynamics). $D_{r}$ is typically set directly in the integrator as rotational diffusion.  \n",
    "\n",
    "Activity (typically quantified by self-propulsion speed or Peclet number) drives persistent motion that breaks equilibrium detailed balance. Higher activity can induce clustering even at densities too low for passive phase separation, triggering motility-induced phase separation. Adding temperature to the simulation alters the effective persistence length of the active particles, which directly influence how long it takes for MIPS to be realized due to increasing (or decreasing) random fluctuations. Using the the OverdampedViscous method in HOOMD-blue, we essentially set the effective temperture of the system to zero. Read more about this integrator in the [HOOMD-blue documentation](https://hoomd-blue.readthedocs.io/en/latest/hoomd/md/methods/overdampedviscous.html). \n",
    "\n",
    "A general rule of thumb is: \n",
    "- Pe < 10: mostly homogeneous, little clustering\n",
    "- Pe ≈ 20–80: MIPS starts to emerge (depending on density)\n",
    "- Pe > 100: strong phase separation likely\n",
    "\n",
    "More information on suggested Pe numbers can be found in [Cates & Tailleur (2015)](https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-conmatphys-031214-014710) and [Marchetti et al. (2023)](https://pubs.rsc.org/en/content/articlelanding/2023/sm/d3sm01059g) for different systems. \n"
    "\n",
    "Below, we set our Peclet number to 20, which will put us in the regime where we can expect MIPS above certain intermediate densities. Since, we have previously set `gamma=1`, we will do this by setting our active force = 2 and rotation diffusion = 0.1, so our peclet number is 20 according to the definition we gave in [section 01 of this tutorial sequence](01-Introduction-to-Active-Particles.ipynb). "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 9,
   "id": "d4e7c9ca-43e6-449b-a564-eebfa7e95f6e",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "activity = 2\n",
    "\n",
    "active = hoomd.md.force.Active(filter=filter_all)\n",
    "active.active_force['A'] = (activity,0,0)\n",
    "active.active_torque['A'] = (0,0,0)\n",
    "\n",
    "integrator.forces.append(active)\n",
    "\n",
    "rotational_diffusion_updater = active.create_diffusion_updater(\n",
    "    trigger=1, rotational_diffusion = 0.1)\n",
    "sim.operations += rotational_diffusion_updater"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "2e714520-cf15-455d-9b08-eb824d616ff2",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Run the Simulation and get an initial snapshot."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 10,
   "id": "d092d62d-a0e3-43f1-a920-a94bd2585592",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "logger = hoomd.logging.Logger(['particle','constraint'])\n",
    "gsd_oper = hoomd.write.GSD(trigger=hoomd.trigger.Periodic(int(100)), filename='MIPS.gsd',logger=logger,mode='wb',dynamic=['momentum','property'],filter=hoomd.filter.All())\n",
    "\n",
    "sim.operations += gsd_oper\n",
    "sim.state.thermalize_particle_momenta(filter=filter_all, kT=kT)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "ea3031df-a4bf-46c9-948e-ac5e8385a536",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Thermalize the system."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 11,
   "id": "1c56e2f6-1865-437c-a9f2-b77026608b8d",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<IPython.core.display.Image object>"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 11,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "sim.run(10000)\n",
    "gsd_oper.flush()\n",
    "render(sim.state.get_snapshot())"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "f90c2fae-e82e-4808-a677-9cf468620208",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Density and 2D MIPS\n",
    "\n",
    "Now that we have set up a 2D simulation of hard spheres of a reasonably high Pe. let number, it is easy to induce MIPS by increasing the system's denisty which affects collision frequency and cluster formation. Increasing density generally promotes phase separation by enhancing particle interactions. MIPS usually emerges above a critical packing fraction around 0.3–0.4 for sufficiently high activity (Peclet number).\n",
    "\n",
    "By systematically varying both density and activity, we can map out the phase diagram and identify regimes where the system spontaneously separates into dense clusters and dilute gas-like regions.\n",
    "\n",
    "For the sake of this tutorial, we will compress our 2D system with Pe=20 to visualize MIPS by following a very similar compression method as in [earlier HOOMD-blue tutorials]((../01-Introducing-Molecular-Dynamics/00-index.ipynb)).\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 12,
   "id": "6b40f4bc-e257-44ca-aad3-bf4c6d93ee2e",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "initial_box = sim.state.box\n",
    "initial_rho = sim.state.N_particles / (initial_box.Lx * initial_box.Ly)\n",
    "\n",
    "final_rho = 0.55\n",
    "final_area = sim.state.N_particles / final_rho\n",
    "final_L = final_area**(1/2)\n",
    "\n",
    "final_box = hoomd.Box.from_box(initial_box)  # make a copy of initial_box\n",
    "final_box.Lx = final_L\n",
    "final_box.Ly = final_L\n",
    "final_box.Lz = 0\n",
    "\n",
    "ramp = hoomd.variant.Ramp(A=0, B=1, t_start=sim.timestep, t_ramp=20000)\n",
    "box_resize_trigger = hoomd.trigger.Periodic(10)\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "interpolated_box = hoomd.variant.box.Interpolate(\n",
    "    initial_box=initial_box, final_box=final_box, variant=ramp)\n",
    "\n",
    "box_resize = hoomd.update.BoxResize(\n",
    "    trigger=hoomd.trigger.Periodic(10),\n",
    "    box=interpolated_box,\n",
    "    filter=hoomd.filter.All()\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "sim.operations.updaters.append(box_resize)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 13,
   "id": "55bcb534-b423-4712-87de-2d9753ac1a65",
   "metadata": {
    "scrolled": true
   },
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<IPython.core.display.Image object>"
      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 13,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "sim.run(200000)\n",
    "gsd_oper.flush()\n",
    "\n",
    "render(sim.state.get_snapshot())"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e129ec47-f879-42c3-b33f-1b08fbeeb6dc",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "By visual inspection, we can see gas-liquid coexistance that we expect from a characteristic MIPS. There are numerous other more quantitive ways to document this behavior as well that we can do using analysis tools like [freud](https://freud.readthedocs.io/en/latest/), for instance."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
